WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.960
One of the trademark texts of the American&nbsp;
school system is Harper Lee’s 1960 novel To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.880
Kill a Mockingbird. For decades it has been widely&nbsp;
read in high schools and middle schools an a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.560
anti-racist text. But how did this novel, with its&nbsp;
Southern Gothic and Bildungsroman elements become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:23.520
a book that in 2006 the British said “every adult&nbsp;
should read before they die” ahead of the Bible.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.840
That’s a flex.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:29.947
[Open]

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:33.760
To Kill a Mockingbird was written by Harper&nbsp;
Lee and was loosely based on Lee’s real life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.560
experiences, the book tells the story of&nbsp;
Jean Louise Finch, better known as Scout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.560
a young girl growing up during the Great&nbsp;
Depression in the fictional town of Maycomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:49.200
Alabama with her older brother Jeremy aka Jem,&nbsp;
and her widowed lawyer father, Atticus Finch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.800
A name, that will be imprinted&nbsp;
on the world … forever.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.960
Atticus is appointed to defend&nbsp;
a Black man named Tom Robinson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.200
who is accused of sexually assaulting&nbsp;
a white woman named Mayella Ewell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.560
This causes a lot of racial harassment towards&nbsp;
the Finch family and at one point in the novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.160
Atticus has to stop an attempted lynching, with&nbsp;
the help of Scout and some of the other children.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.360
Scout and Jem watch the trial from the&nbsp;
Black section of the courtroom. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.480
revealed that Mayella is lying and attempted&nbsp;
to seduce Tom, but was beaten by her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.360
who has been sexually abusing his daughter.&nbsp;
Despite being labeled as “white trash”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.720
Tom is convicted for the assault and is&nbsp;
later killed while trying to escape jail.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.680
Mayella’s father, Bob, tries to get revenge&nbsp;
on Atticus by attacking Jem and Scout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.600
but are saved by the town recluse Boo Radley.&nbsp;
Bob is killed by a knife wound from Radley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.280
but it is covered up and Scout is&nbsp;
forever changed by this experience.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.240
So who was the mind behind the mockingbird?

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.000
Born Nelle Harper Lee in Monroeville, Alabama,&nbsp;
she was also the young daughter of a lawyer father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.680
named Amasa Coleman Lee.
 
One of her childhood friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.400
and long time writing companion was Truman&nbsp;
Capote, who visited their town during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.480
summers. They would end up living near&nbsp;
each other years later in New York City.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.480
After publishing Mockingbird, Lee&nbsp;
went on a bunch of book tours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.880
worked on the film adaptation of her book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.240
and then just went on to lead the successful&nbsp;
life of someone who published one awesome book.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:29.600
Just one.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:30.880
One book.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:32.480
No sequels.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.120
[oh fortuna]

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.560
The success of To Kill a Mockingbird was instant&nbsp;
and surpassed the expectations of Lee tenfold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.840
Professor Alice Hall Petry&nbsp;
wrote in the introduction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.720
On Harper Lee: Essays and Reflections:

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.080
“During the eighty-year period between&nbsp;
1895 and 1975, Mockingbird was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.200
third-best-selling novel in the United States,&nbsp;
and the seventh-best-selling book of any kind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.440
Sales have not fallen off in&nbsp;
the ensuing twenty-five years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.880
In 2001, for example, more than 1&nbsp;
million copies of Mockingbird were sold.”

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.360
It has been kept alive through it’s pop&nbsp;
culture influence via the film adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.280
and being a regularly included text&nbsp;
for middle school children. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.360
just because a book is taught at school&nbsp;
doesn’t automatically make it popular.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.320
People aren’t this interested in&nbsp;
something like The Scarlet Letter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.440
and even that wasn’t as huge of&nbsp;
a success when it was released.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.040
UNC chapel hill Professor Emerita Doris&nbsp;
Betts ties it’s longevity to the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:35.120
simplistic morality of the text in her essay “The&nbsp;
Mockingbird’s Throat: A Personal Reflection”:

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.560
“In Scout Finch, Lee has produced a girl&nbsp;
as appealing as Jo March of Little Women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.520
but stopped her story sooner than Louisa&nbsp;
May Alcott stopped hers, and the point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:52.800
initiation became new maturity. By producing no&nbsp;
other novels, Lee also has left readers feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:04:00.320
that this book has particular significance in that&nbsp;
it contains all she had to say, or all she could.”

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.440
And with all of that symbolism and light&nbsp;
discussion of race it raises the question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.960
what exactly is the value of&nbsp;
teaching To Kill A Mockingbird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:14.960
versus an actual book about&nbsp;
race written by a Black author?

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.400
Gerald Early, professor of&nbsp;
African American Studies, states:

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.000
“I read To Kill a Mockingbird the summer before&nbsp;
I read Richard Wright’s Uncle Tom’s Children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.080
a famous collection of stories by a&nbsp;
black writer about race in the South.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.440
The difference was, of course, astonishing because&nbsp;
the perspectives were so clearly different[…]

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.680
The Wright stories taught me two&nbsp;
contradictory but inescapable facts:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.360
first, that books like To Kill a&nbsp;
Mockingbird were inadequate for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.560
as a black reader and failed, in their&nbsp;
fumbling literal way, to give me black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.280
people as I knew them to be; and second, that&nbsp;
I needed books like To Kill a Mockingbird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.880
if ever I were going to understand the South&nbsp;
as a construction of the white southern mind.”

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.880
To Kill A Mockingbird offers some&nbsp;
semblance of safety for white readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.960
who do not want to feel alienated or&nbsp;
called out by an anti-racist text.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:10.000
They can see Scout and her father, Atticus,&nbsp;
as a fair depiction of Southern progressivism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.640
and put the legacy of racism and all&nbsp;
it’s terrors on “other white people.”

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.680
But it does that, as Professor Isaac Saney writes&nbsp;
in “The Case Against To Kill a Mockingbird”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.520
by making Atticus a white savior&nbsp;
and turning the Black characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.800
and their community into pawns on Scout’s journey.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.160
“Perhaps the most egregious&nbsp;
characteristic of the novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.880
is the denial of the historical agency of&nbsp;
Black people. They are robbed of their role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.760
as subjects of history, reduced to mere&nbsp;
objects who are passive hapless victims;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:46.560
mere spectators and bystanders in the struggle&nbsp;
against their own oppression and exploitation.”

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.800
Which brings us to the complex&nbsp;
issues of Atticus Finch himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.920
one of literatures great white heroes.&nbsp;
What does the role he plays in the books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:02.160
actually say about the role white people&nbsp;
play as allies? Well, not much of anything.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.600
According to bad tweeter&nbsp;
Malcolm Gladwell in his essay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.840
“The Courthouse Ring: Atticus Finch&nbsp;
and the limits of Southern liberalism”:

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:15.120
“Finch will stand up to racists. He’ll use his&nbsp;
moral authority to shame them into silence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.320
He will leave the judge standing on the&nbsp;
sidewalk while he shakes hands with Negroes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.360
What he will not do is look at the problem&nbsp;
of racism outside the immediate context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.960
of Mr. Cunningham, Mr. Levy, and the&nbsp;
island community of Maycomb, Alabama.”

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.160
Atticus delivering an inspired defense of Tom&nbsp;
Robinson and protecting him has positioned him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:43.680
especially in the eyes of white audiences,&nbsp;
as the perfect ally. Until Go Set A Watchman.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:50.400
And it is at this point I am obligated&nbsp;
to have to discuss Go Set A Watchman.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.640
Remember how Harper Lee only had that one&nbsp;
awesome book? Well, that changed in 2015&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.160
when Go Set A Watchman was released. The book’s&nbsp;
road to publication was a controversial one.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.320
The novel was “re-discovered” and Lee&nbsp;
released a statement with Harper Collins:

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.320
"In the mid-1950s, I completed a novel&nbsp;
called Go Set a Watchman. It features the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.760
character known as Scout as an adult woman and&nbsp;
I thought it a pretty decent effort. My editor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:22.800
who was taken by the flashbacks to Scout's&nbsp;
childhood, persuaded me to write a novel from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.680
the point of view of the young Scout. I was&nbsp;
a first-time writer, so I did as I was told.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.200
I hadn't realized it had survived, so was&nbsp;
surprised and delighted when my dear friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.960
and lawyer Tonja Carter discovered it. After&nbsp;
much thought and hesitation I shared it with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.840
handful of people I trust and was pleased to hear&nbsp;
that they considered it worthy of publication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.920
I am humbled and amazed that this will&nbsp;
now be published after all these years."

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.840
Some argued that this “re-discovery”&nbsp;
was suspicious because there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.960
conflicting reports about the&nbsp;
state of Lee’s mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.840
and if this was ever meant to be published.&nbsp;
But people were excited. I mean it is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.640
sequel to one of the most popular books&nbsp;
of all time … why wouldn’t they publish?

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.000
And then it was bad. Like really, really bad.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.200
Alexandra Petri of the Washington&nbsp;
Post said in her review:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:21.600
“It’s the Jar Jar of classic literature: It&nbsp;
takes place in a beloved fictional universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:28.080
and it’s uncomfortably racist. It has, at best,&nbsp;
a scholarly interest. I can’t imagine that Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:33.120
would want it to represent her in the world.&nbsp;
I don’t think it should have been published.”

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.920
Michiko Kakutani for The New York Times&nbsp;
specifically addressed in her piece the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:44.280
point that got people upset: the fact that&nbsp;
their beloved Atticus Finch was … racist.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:50.640
“[...] Atticus is a racist who once attended a&nbsp;
Klan meeting, who says things like “The Negroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:56.320
down here are still in their childhood as a&nbsp;
people.” Or asks his daughter: “Do you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.400
Negroes by the carload in our schools and churches&nbsp;
and theaters? Do you want them in our world?”

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:07.680
In “Mockingbird,” a book once described&nbsp;
by Oprah Winfrey as “our national novel,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.520
Atticus praised American courts as&nbsp;
“the great levelers,” dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.560
the proposition that “all men are created&nbsp;
equal.” In “Watchman,” set in the 1950s in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.440
the era of the Brown v. Board of Education&nbsp;
decision, he denounces the Supreme Court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:28.240
says he wants his home state “to be left alone&nbsp;
to keep house without advice from the N.A.A.C.P.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:33.680
and describes N.A.A.C.P.-paid lawyers&nbsp;
as “standing around like buzzards.”

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.840
This was a really awkward moment to be an Atticus.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.760
People were betrayed. It was the last season&nbsp;
of Game of Thrones levels of distraught.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.160
Their Khaleesi turned fascist so&nbsp;
quickly and without warning. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.560
Some people, like Naa Baako Ako-Adjei, a&nbsp;
writer and educator, saw this as another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:56.640
reason why To Kill A Mockingbird is not that&nbsp;
ideal of a text to teach about anti-racism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.320
Not simply because it was a very white voice&nbsp;
on Black oppression, but also elements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:08.320
classism on which kind of white people are to&nbsp;
“blame” for the situation in Jim Crow America:

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.200
“...Lee is certainly not the first Southerner&nbsp;
to blame poor whites for racist violence in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.880
South; it’s a practice with its own long&nbsp;
history. In 1932, the well-known liberal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.480
Southern journalist, Gerald White Johnson, wrote&nbsp;
in the then influential North American Review&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:28.480
that “Southerners with money&nbsp;
in the bank do not lynch”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:34.400
and that lynch mobs were usually composed of&nbsp;
“fellows who have not two nickels to rub together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:39.840
those inexpressibly forlorn outcasts known&nbsp;
in the South as the poor white trash.”

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.560
...There are, however, moments in the novel&nbsp;
in which you see Lee falter in her attempts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:51.040
to make only poor whites responsible for the&nbsp;
region’s race problem and absolve ‘fine families’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:56.640
for the nature of things in the South when she&nbsp;
acknowledges that both rich and poor whites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:02.320
mistreat black people. “As you grow older,”&nbsp;
Atticus tells Jem, “you’ll see white men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:08.000
cheat black men every day of your life, but let&nbsp;
me tell you something and don’t forget it—whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.200
a white man does that to a black man,&nbsp;
no matter who he is, how rich he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.800
or how fine a family he comes from, that&nbsp;
white man is trash” (p. 233). But in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.760
Lee reverts back to scapegoating Bob&nbsp;
Ewell and other poor, ‘white trash.’”

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.680
For so long, Mockingbird was able to give&nbsp;
white readers a story to teach them about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:32.320
racism, but also remove them from any&nbsp;
accountability of perpetuating that system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.320
They had Atticus to show them that it wasn't&nbsp;
all of them, and Watchman took that away.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:42.160
When I finally read To Kill a Mockingbird,&nbsp;
for myself in college, I found it a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:47.920
readable novel and I got through it fairly&nbsp;
quickly. While well written, and definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:49.920
good, I found myself glad I didn’t read it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:56.240
for school because the question I found&nbsp;
myself asking is: who is this for?

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.800
It is not without merit, but it doesn’t&nbsp;
center Black characters or Black experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:06.240
it simply observes them through Scout. It is&nbsp;
an introduction to empathy but at a distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:11.200
and that layer of separation is deeply felt.&nbsp;
At least to me. I don’t think we need to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.360
teaching To Kill a Mockingbird, but I think&nbsp;
it should be followed up with a Bildungsroman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:19.760
that actually puts you in the space of a&nbsp;
Black person, rather than a white child.

